    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Franks emerged on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhine">Rhine</a> in the middle of the 3rd century, not as a migrating people but as a new grouping of older peoples who had long lived beside the Roman frontier, among them the Chamavi, Chattuarii, Bructeri, Amsivarii and Tencteri, and perhaps the Sugambri. The name, which first appears in Roman sources in connection with raids into Gaul in the 250s, is usually explained as ‘the bold’ or ‘the fierce’; the later meaning ‘free’ derived from the people’s name, not the reverse. Like the Alemanni further south, the Franks took shape as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>’s frontier weakened during the crisis of the 3rd century, when emperors fought one another and the Rhine garrisons were reduced. Frankish bands crossed into Gaul by land and raided by sea along the coasts of Gaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>, and some are said to have reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and sacked Tarraco in the 260s. Later Frankish tradition, recorded in the 7th-century chronicle of Fredegar, claimed descent from the Trojans, a learned legend that placed the Franks on a level with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a>. Early leaders included kings such as Gennobaudes, who submitted to the emperor Maximian around 288 and received his kingdom back as a Roman client.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#cologne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cologne.jpg" alt="Cologne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kranh%C3%A4user_Cologne,_April_2018_-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Falbisoner, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Frankish society on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhine">Rhine</a> was made up of farming communities in a landscape of river valleys, heath and coastal marsh, where families lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a> longhouses, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and grew cereals. Status rested on kinship, land, cattle and service in the war bands of kings and nobles, and later Frankish law, the <em>Pactus Legis Salicae</em> issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, reflects this society in its tariffs of compensation, or wergild, for the killing of free Franks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a> and the half-free. Contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> shaped every part of Frankish life. Roman coins, glassware, bronze vessels and pottery reached Frankish settlements through trade, plunder and soldiers’ pay, and from the late 3rd century many Franks lived inside the empire as <em>laeti</em>, settled farmer-soldiers, or later as federates in northern Gaul. In Toxandria, the Salian Franks farmed land between the Meuse and the Scheldt under Roman authority. Cemeteries such as Krefeld-Gellep on the Rhine show Frankish and Roman burial customs mixing, with weapons placed beside Roman goods. Most Franks remained pagan until the conversion of Clovis around 500, worshipping Germanic gods, although some Franks in Roman service were Christians, and the Rhineland cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trier">Trier</a> kept their Christian communities under Frankish rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains.jpg" alt="Battle of the Catalaunian Plains" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thur%C3%B3czy_kr%C3%B3nika_-_Attila_kir%C3%A1ly_csat%C3%A1ja_a_catalaunumi_s%C3%ADkon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johannes de Thurocz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Frankish warfare combined raids by small bands with service in Roman armies. In the 3rd century Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#piracy">pirates</a> ravaged the coasts of Gaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>; Carausius was given command of the Channel fleet against Frankish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> raiders around 285, and a famous story tells of Frankish prisoners settled by Probus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> who seized ships and sailed home through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The emperors answered with punishing campaigns: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> threw the captured Frankish kings Ascaric and Merogaisus to wild beasts in the arena at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trier">Trier</a> around 306, and Constans fought the Franks in 341–342. In 355 Franks sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a>, which Julian recovered in 356, and in 358 he defeated the Chamavi and accepted the surrender of the Salians. Frankish warriors fought on foot with spear, shield and sword, the throwing axe later called the <em>francisca</em> and the barbed throwing spear, the <em>angon</em>. In Roman service they reached the highest commands, and the Frankish king Mallobaudes helped win the Battle of Argentaria in 378. Frankish federates fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vandals">Vandals</a> as they approached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhine">Rhine</a> in 406, and Frankish troops served under Aetius against Attila at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a> in 451, while other Franks backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huns">Huns</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clovis-i.jpg" alt="Clovis I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Remy_baptise_Clovis_d%C3%A9tail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pethrus (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There was no single Frankish state before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis I</a>. Roman writers speak of several kings, each with his own people and war band, and by the 5th century two main groupings can be distinguished: the Salian Franks of the north-west, in Toxandria and later around Tournai and Cambrai, and the Rhineland Franks, later called Ripuarians, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a>. Kings came from leading families who claimed noble or sacred descent; the long hair of the Merovingian kings later served as a mark of royal blood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> managed its relations with these rulers through treaties, hostages, settlements of <em>laeti</em> and the recruitment of Frankish leaders into the imperial elite. In the late 4th century Franks such as Bauto, Richomer and Arbogast, and probably Merobaudes, became commanders of the Roman army, several of them consuls, and Bauto’s daughter Aelia Eudoxia married the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arcadius">Arcadius</a>. In the 5th century Frankish kings ruled Roman provincials as well as Franks. Chlodio took Cambrai and the land up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-somme">Somme</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#childeric-i">Childeric I</a>, father of Clovis, appears both as a king of the Franks and as a holder of Roman authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gallia-belgica">Belgica</a> Secunda, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">Bishop</a> Remigius of Reims implied when he wrote to Clovis on his accession. Childeric’s grave at Tournai, found in 1653, contained a signet ring inscribed CHILDERICI REGIS and a Roman officer’s gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crossbow">crossbow</a> brooch.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#frankfurt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankfurt.jpg" alt="Frankfurt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#frankfurt">Frankfurt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankfurt_am_Main,_Central_business_district_(2024).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dr. Thomas Liptak, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Frankish confederation was the seedbed of the most durable of the successor kingdoms of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Unlike the Goths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vandals">Vandals</a>, who migrated over long distances, the Franks expanded outward from their old homes into neighbouring Roman provinces, keeping a base on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhine">Rhine</a> while absorbing the Gallo-Roman population of northern Gaul. Long service in the Roman army gave Frankish leaders experience of Roman command, law and administration, which helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis</a> and his successors rule a mixed population with the help of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> and officials. The Merovingian kingdom founded by Clovis, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> that followed it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, grew from this base, and their divisions produced the kingdoms that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a>. The name of the Franks survives in France, in the German region of Franconia and in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, the ‘ford of the Franks’. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabs">Arabs</a> came to use ‘Franks’ for western Europeans in general, and in the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">Crusades</a> the Arabic <em>Ifranj</em> or <em>Firanj</em> was applied to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latins">Latin Christians</a>. The Frankish language left many words in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#old-french">Old French</a> and so in modern French, including <em>guerre</em> (war) and <em>jardin</em> (garden), and its Low Franconian dialects developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#childeric-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/childeric-i.jpg" alt="Childeric I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#childeric-i">Childeric I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHILDERICI_REGIS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The confederation did not collapse but was transformed into a kingdom. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#childeric-i">Childeric I</a> died around 481, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, then about fifteen, succeeded him as king of the Salian Franks of Tournai, one of several Frankish kings. In 486 Clovis defeated Syagrius, ruler of the last Roman enclave in northern Gaul, at Soissons, and Syagrius was later handed over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visigoths">Visigoths</a> and executed. Clovis then defeated the Alemanni, converted to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a>, being baptised at Reims by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">Bishop</a> Remigius on a date variously placed between 496 and 508, and in 507 defeated the Visigoths at the Battle of Vouillé, taking most of Aquitaine. In his last years he eliminated the other Frankish kings, including Sigibert the Lame of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a> and his son Chloderic and the kings Chararic and Ragnachar, so that by his death in 511 the Franks were under one ruler, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> as his chief seat. In this way the many kingships of the confederation ended, replaced by a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>. Clovis’s kingdom was divided among his four sons, but the idea of one Frankish people under one royal family endured, and the Merovingians ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a> until 751.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Franks were the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhine">Rhine</a> confederation whose long pressure on, and service in, the Roman frontier prepared the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clovis-i">Clovis I</a> to unite them and conquer Gaul, founding the Frankish kingdom from which both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> grew and giving their name to France, Franconia and western Europeans generally.</em></p>

